{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder asks the expiry cleanup trigger under canonical reservation staging. Existing contract TimedRule (packages/contract/src/definition.ts:33-39) has only effects and attention; PlatformData inherits it, and packages/contract/src/platform.ts:196-200 explicitly says a timed rule holds no mark and its effects are total. It has no send or operation-opening form. A staged reservation that receives a configured failure or an authorized cancel can open recorded reservation-delete/mint/revoke during that deciding input. A timeout only changes its publication item; it cannot automatically open deletion or mutate the branch. The current @3 candidate therefore leaves expiry cleanup-aborted live, preserving its held cleanup/custody, while the lane merge times out and the next reservation can reclaim logical branch availability. Proposed concrete trigger: broaden the existing authorized resend act on that cleanup-aborted publication to open its recorded deletion and mint; alarms then drive those operations and revoke their tokens normally. This adds no user act name, host write hidden in a read, or invented timed mark. Planner judgment requested: does explicit resend satisfy the expiry cleanup duty, or is an owner-adopted automatic maintenance message/form required? Until decided, explicit resend is a source candidate, not accepted completion. Publication, cancellation and failure cleanup implementation and focused tests continue independently.","body":{"item":"expired-reservation-cleanup-trigger","to":"planner"}}